{"website":{"seo":{"siteTitle":"","metaDescription":"","defaultKeywords":[],"canonicalBase":"","ogImage":"","twitterHandle":"","googleSiteVerification":"","bingSiteVerification":"","googleAnalyticsId":"","googleTagManagerId":""},"technical":{"robotsTxt":"User-agent: *\nAllow: \/","htaccess":"","blogPath":"\/blog","includeBlogsInSitemap":true},"_id":"6abd45a45508bfc63573f7c1","name":"hits9","domain":"hits9.com","description":"","platform":"coded","active":true,"faviconUrl":"","createdAt":"2026-09-30T17:23:48.748Z","__v":0},"blogs":[{"cta":{"label":"Get Quote","url":"\/contact"},"_id":"6ac8bbac01767108a44c98a1","websiteId":"6abd45a45508bfc63573f7c1","title":"Top 20 Iron Rich Foods to Increase Hemoglobin Naturally and Improve Health","slug":"iron-rich-foods-to-increase-hemoglobin","content":"<p>Iron&nbsp;is&nbsp;an&nbsp;essential&nbsp;mineral&nbsp;that&nbsp;plays&nbsp;a&nbsp;vital&nbsp;role&nbsp;in&nbsp;producing&nbsp;<strong>hemoglobin<\/strong>,&nbsp;the&nbsp;protein&nbsp;in&nbsp;red&nbsp;blood&nbsp;cells&nbsp;responsible&nbsp;for&nbsp;carrying&nbsp;oxygen&nbsp;throughout&nbsp;the&nbsp;body.&nbsp;When&nbsp;your&nbsp;body&nbsp;doesn&#39;t&nbsp;get&nbsp;enough&nbsp;iron,&nbsp;hemoglobin&nbsp;levels&nbsp;can&nbsp;drop,&nbsp;leading&nbsp;to&nbsp;<strong>iron&nbsp;deficiency&nbsp;anemia<\/strong>,&nbsp;fatigue,&nbsp;weakness,&nbsp;dizziness,&nbsp;poor&nbsp;concentration,&nbsp;and&nbsp;reduced&nbsp;immunity.<\/p><p><\/p><p>The&nbsp;good&nbsp;news&nbsp;is&nbsp;that&nbsp;many&nbsp;everyday&nbsp;foods&nbsp;are&nbsp;naturally&nbsp;rich&nbsp;in&nbsp;iron.&nbsp;By&nbsp;including&nbsp;these&nbsp;foods&nbsp;in&nbsp;your&nbsp;daily&nbsp;diet&nbsp;and&nbsp;pairing&nbsp;them&nbsp;with&nbsp;vitamin&nbsp;C-rich&nbsp;fruits&nbsp;and&nbsp;vegetables,&nbsp;you&nbsp;can&nbsp;improve&nbsp;iron&nbsp;absorption&nbsp;and&nbsp;support&nbsp;healthy&nbsp;hemoglobin&nbsp;levels&nbsp;naturally.<\/p><p><\/p><h2><strong>Why&nbsp;Is&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;Important?<\/strong><\/h2><p><\/p><p>Iron&nbsp;is&nbsp;essential&nbsp;for&nbsp;many&nbsp;important&nbsp;functions&nbsp;in&nbsp;the&nbsp;body,&nbsp;including:<\/p><p><\/p><ul><li>Helps&nbsp;produce&nbsp;healthy&nbsp;red&nbsp;blood&nbsp;cells<\/li><li>Improves&nbsp;hemoglobin&nbsp;levels<\/li><li>Carries&nbsp;oxygen&nbsp;throughout&nbsp;the&nbsp;body<\/li><li>Supports&nbsp;energy&nbsp;production<\/li><li>Strengthens&nbsp;the&nbsp;immune&nbsp;system<\/li><li>Enhances&nbsp;brain&nbsp;function&nbsp;and&nbsp;concentration<\/li><li>Reduces&nbsp;fatigue&nbsp;and&nbsp;weakness<\/li><li>Promotes&nbsp;healthy&nbsp;pregnancy&nbsp;and&nbsp;child&nbsp;development<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><h2>Top&nbsp;20&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;Rich&nbsp;Foods<\/h2><p><\/p><p><strong>1.&nbsp;Spinach<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;2.7&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Rich&nbsp;in&nbsp;iron,&nbsp;folate&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;antioxidants<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Salads,&nbsp;soups,&nbsp;smoothies<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>2.&nbsp;Lentils&nbsp;(Dal)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;3.3&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Excellent&nbsp;vegetarian&nbsp;protein&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;iron<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Dal,&nbsp;soups,&nbsp;curry<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>3.&nbsp;Chickpeas&nbsp;(Chana)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;2.9&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Supports&nbsp;healthy&nbsp;blood&nbsp;production<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Boiled,&nbsp;roasted,&nbsp;hummus<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>4.&nbsp;Kidney&nbsp;Beans&nbsp;(Rajma)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;2.9&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;High&nbsp;in&nbsp;iron&nbsp;and&nbsp;fiber<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Rajma&nbsp;curry,&nbsp;salads<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>5.&nbsp;Soybeans<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;5.1&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Rich&nbsp;in&nbsp;protein&nbsp;and&nbsp;iron<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Soy&nbsp;chunks,&nbsp;tofu,&nbsp;soy&nbsp;milk<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>6.&nbsp;Tofu<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;5.4&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Great&nbsp;vegan&nbsp;protein&nbsp;source<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Stir-fries,&nbsp;grilled<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>7.&nbsp;Pumpkin&nbsp;Seeds<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;8.8&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;High&nbsp;in&nbsp;iron,&nbsp;zinc&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;magnesium<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Snack,&nbsp;salads<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>8.&nbsp;Sesame&nbsp;Seeds&nbsp;(Til)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;14.6&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Excellent&nbsp;plant-based&nbsp;iron&nbsp;source<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Sprinkle&nbsp;on&nbsp;meals<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>9.&nbsp;Beetroot<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;0.8&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Supports&nbsp;blood&nbsp;formation<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Juice,&nbsp;salad<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>10.&nbsp;Broccoli<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;0.7&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Contains&nbsp;iron&nbsp;and&nbsp;vitamin&nbsp;C<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Steamed,&nbsp;stir-fried<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>11.&nbsp;Quinoa<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;1.5&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Complete&nbsp;protein&nbsp;with&nbsp;iron<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Salads,&nbsp;bowls<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>12.&nbsp;Dates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;1.0&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Natural&nbsp;energy&nbsp;booster<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Snack,&nbsp;smoothies<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>13.&nbsp;Raisins<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;1.9&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Good&nbsp;source&nbsp;of&nbsp;iron&nbsp;and&nbsp;antioxidants<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Breakfast,&nbsp;trail&nbsp;mix<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>14.&nbsp;Pomegranate<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;0.3&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Supports&nbsp;healthy&nbsp;blood&nbsp;circulation<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Fresh&nbsp;fruit,&nbsp;juice<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>15.&nbsp;Eggs<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;1.8&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Protein&nbsp;with&nbsp;moderate&nbsp;iron<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Boiled,&nbsp;omelet<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>16.&nbsp;Chicken&nbsp;Liver<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;9.0&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;One&nbsp;of&nbsp;the&nbsp;richest&nbsp;heme&nbsp;iron&nbsp;foods<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Stir-fried<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>17.&nbsp;Lean&nbsp;Red&nbsp;Meat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;2.6&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Highly&nbsp;absorbable&nbsp;heme&nbsp;iron<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Grilled,&nbsp;roasted<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>18.&nbsp;Fish&nbsp;(Tuna,&nbsp;Sardines)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;1\u20132&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;plus&nbsp;omega-3&nbsp;fatty&nbsp;acids<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Baked,&nbsp;grilled<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>19.&nbsp;Shellfish&nbsp;(Clams,&nbsp;Oysters)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;Up&nbsp;to&nbsp;28&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Extremely&nbsp;rich&nbsp;in&nbsp;iron<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Steamed<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p><strong>20.&nbsp;Dark&nbsp;Chocolate&nbsp;(70%+)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Iron:&nbsp;11.9&nbsp;mg&nbsp;per&nbsp;100g<\/li><li>Major&nbsp;Benefits:&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;with&nbsp;powerful&nbsp;antioxidants<\/li><li>Best&nbsp;Way&nbsp;to&nbsp;Eat:&nbsp;Moderate&nbsp;consumption<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><h3><strong>Daily&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;Requirements<\/strong><\/h3><p><\/p><p><strong>Age&nbsp;Group<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Recommended&nbsp;Daily&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;Intake<\/strong><\/p><p>Adult&nbsp;Men<\/p><p><strong>8&nbsp;mg<\/strong><\/p><p>Women&nbsp;(19\u201350&nbsp;years)<\/p><p><strong>18&nbsp;mg<\/strong><\/p><p>Women&nbsp;(51+&nbsp;years)<\/p><p><strong>8&nbsp;mg<\/strong><\/p><p>Pregnant&nbsp;Women<\/p><p><strong>27&nbsp;mg<\/strong><\/p><p>Breastfeeding&nbsp;Women<\/p><p><strong>9\u201310&nbsp;mg<\/strong><\/p><p>Children&nbsp;(1\u20138&nbsp;years)<\/p><p><strong>7\u201310&nbsp;mg<\/strong><\/p><p>Teenagers<\/p><p><strong>11\u201315&nbsp;mg<\/strong><\/p><p><\/p><h3><strong>Tips&nbsp;to&nbsp;Increase&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;Absorption<\/strong><\/h3><p><\/p><p>To&nbsp;get&nbsp;the&nbsp;maximum&nbsp;benefit&nbsp;from&nbsp;iron-rich&nbsp;foods,&nbsp;follow&nbsp;these&nbsp;simple&nbsp;tips:<\/p><p><\/p><ul><li>Eat&nbsp;vitamin&nbsp;C-rich&nbsp;foods&nbsp;like&nbsp;oranges,&nbsp;lemons,&nbsp;kiwi,&nbsp;strawberries,&nbsp;tomatoes,&nbsp;and&nbsp;bell&nbsp;peppers&nbsp;with&nbsp;iron-rich&nbsp;meals.<\/li><li>Avoid&nbsp;drinking&nbsp;tea&nbsp;or&nbsp;coffee&nbsp;for&nbsp;at&nbsp;least&nbsp;one&nbsp;hour&nbsp;before&nbsp;or&nbsp;after&nbsp;meals.<\/li><li>Include&nbsp;both&nbsp;plant-based&nbsp;and&nbsp;animal-based&nbsp;iron&nbsp;sources&nbsp;in&nbsp;your&nbsp;diet.<\/li><li>Cook&nbsp;food&nbsp;in&nbsp;cast&nbsp;iron&nbsp;cookware&nbsp;whenever&nbsp;possible.<\/li><li>Maintain&nbsp;adequate&nbsp;intake&nbsp;of&nbsp;vitamin&nbsp;B12,&nbsp;folate,&nbsp;and&nbsp;protein.<\/li><li>Soak,&nbsp;sprout,&nbsp;or&nbsp;ferment&nbsp;legumes&nbsp;to&nbsp;improve&nbsp;iron&nbsp;absorption.<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><h3><strong>Signs&nbsp;of&nbsp;Low&nbsp;Hemoglobin&nbsp;or&nbsp;Iron&nbsp;Deficiency<\/strong><\/h3><p><\/p><p>Low&nbsp;iron&nbsp;levels&nbsp;can&nbsp;cause&nbsp;several&nbsp;symptoms,&nbsp;including:<\/p><p><\/p><ul><li>Constant&nbsp;fatigue<\/li><li>Weakness<\/li><li>Pale&nbsp;skin<\/li><li>Dizziness<\/li><li>Shortness&nbsp;of&nbsp;breath<\/li><li>Frequent&nbsp;headaches<\/li><li>Cold&nbsp;hands&nbsp;and&nbsp;feet<\/li><li>Brittle&nbsp;nails<\/li><li>Hair&nbsp;loss<\/li><li>Poor&nbsp;concentration<\/li><li>Rapid&nbsp;heartbeat<\/li><li>Reduced&nbsp;immunity<\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><p>If&nbsp;these&nbsp;symptoms&nbsp;continue,&nbsp;consult&nbsp;a&nbsp;healthcare&nbsp;professional&nbsp;for&nbsp;proper&nbsp;diagnosis&nbsp;and&nbsp;treatment.<\/p><p><\/p><h3><strong>Conclusion<\/strong><\/h3><p><\/p><p>Including&nbsp;a&nbsp;variety&nbsp;of&nbsp;<strong>iron-rich&nbsp;foods<\/strong>&nbsp;in&nbsp;your&nbsp;daily&nbsp;meals&nbsp;is&nbsp;one&nbsp;of&nbsp;the&nbsp;best&nbsp;natural&nbsp;ways&nbsp;to&nbsp;improve&nbsp;hemoglobin&nbsp;levels,&nbsp;prevent&nbsp;iron&nbsp;deficiency,&nbsp;and&nbsp;maintain&nbsp;good&nbsp;overall&nbsp;health.&nbsp;Foods&nbsp;like&nbsp;<strong>spinach,&nbsp;lentils,&nbsp;soybeans,&nbsp;pumpkin&nbsp;seeds,&nbsp;sesame&nbsp;seeds,&nbsp;tofu,&nbsp;chicken&nbsp;liver,&nbsp;lean&nbsp;red&nbsp;meat,&nbsp;fish,&nbsp;and&nbsp;shellfish<\/strong>&nbsp;provide&nbsp;valuable&nbsp;iron&nbsp;along&nbsp;with&nbsp;essential&nbsp;vitamins&nbsp;and&nbsp;minerals&nbsp;that&nbsp;support&nbsp;healthy&nbsp;blood&nbsp;production.<\/p><p><\/p><p>For&nbsp;the&nbsp;best&nbsp;results,&nbsp;pair&nbsp;iron-rich&nbsp;foods&nbsp;with&nbsp;<strong>vitamin&nbsp;C-rich&nbsp;fruits&nbsp;and&nbsp;vegetables<\/strong>,&nbsp;stay&nbsp;hydrated,&nbsp;and&nbsp;follow&nbsp;a&nbsp;balanced&nbsp;diet.&nbsp;If&nbsp;you&nbsp;continue&nbsp;to&nbsp;experience&nbsp;symptoms&nbsp;of&nbsp;low&nbsp;hemoglobin&nbsp;despite&nbsp;dietary&nbsp;improvements,&nbsp;seek&nbsp;medical&nbsp;advice&nbsp;for&nbsp;proper&nbsp;evaluation&nbsp;and&nbsp;treatment.<\/p><p><\/p>","excerpt":"","featuredImage":"","featuredImageAlt":"","images":[],"tags":[],"categoryId":{"_id":"6ac1f2b801767108a445e01f","name":"Health & Fitness","slug":"health-fitness","status":"active"},"authorName":"Admin","publishDate":"2026-10-09T00:00:00.000Z","focusKeyword":"Iron rich foods to increase hemoglobin","secondaryKeywords":["best iron rich foods for low hemoglobin","iron rich foods for vegetarians","foods to increase hemoglobin naturally","iron rich fruits and vegetables","vegetarian foods high in iron","iron rich foods for anemia","best foods to improve iron levels","foods high in iron and vitamin C","daily iron intake for adults","natural ways to improve hemoglobin","iron rich seeds and nuts","iron absorption tips for better health"],"metaTitle":"20 Iron Rich Foods to Increase Hemoglobin and Improve Your Health","metaDescription":"Discover 20 iron rich foods to support healthy hemoglobin levels, including vegetarian sources, fruits, seeds and iron absorption tips for better overall health.","metaKeyword":"best iron rich foods for low hemoglobin, iron rich foods for vegetarians, foods to increase hemoglobin naturally, iron rich fruits and vegetables, vegetarian foods high in iron, iron rich foods for anemia, best foods to improve iron levels, foods high in iron and vitamin C, daily iron intake for adults, natural ways to improve hemoglobin, iron rich seeds and nuts, iron absorption tips for better health","canonicalUrl":"","ogTitle":"Top 20 Iron Rich Foods to Increase Hemoglobin Naturally and Improve Health","ogDescription":"","ogImage":"","schemaType":"BlogPosting","robotsIndex":"index","robotsFollow":"follow","faqs":[{"question":"Which food contains the highest amount of iron?","answer":"Shellfish such as oysters and clams contain some of the highest amounts of naturally occurring iron. Among vegetarian foods, sesame seeds and pumpkin seeds are excellent choices.","_id":"6ac8bbac01767108a44c98a2"},{"question":"Which fruit is best for increasing hemoglobin?","answer":"Pomegranate, dates, raisins, dried apricots, and vitamin C-rich fruits like oranges and kiwi are beneficial for supporting healthy hemoglobin levels.","_id":"6ac8bbac01767108a44c98a3"},{"question":"Is spinach enough to treat iron deficiency?","answer":"Spinach is a healthy source of iron, but it should be combined with other iron-rich foods and vitamin C sources for better absorption. Severe iron deficiency may require medical treatment.","_id":"6ac8bbac01767108a44c98a4"},{"question":"How can I improve iron absorption naturally?","answer":"Consume iron-rich foods together with vitamin C-rich fruits and vegetables, avoid tea and coffee around mealtime, and maintain a balanced diet containing protein, folate, and vitamin B12.","_id":"6ac8bbac01767108a44c98a5"}],"relatedProducts":[],"relatedBlogs":[],"youtubeUrl":"","internalLinks":[],"externalLinks":[],"status":"published","publishedAt":"2026-10-09T10:02:20.226Z","createdAt":"2026-10-09T10:02:20.228Z","__v":0,"category":{"id":"6ac1f2b801767108a445e01f","name":"Health & Fitness","slug":"health-fitness"}}],"pagination":{"page":1,"limit":9,"total":1,"pages":1}}